le: Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\nTitle: Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\nAuthors: David R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\nDate: Sat Oct 19 15:48:04 2013\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1310.5240v1)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n## Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nDavid R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\n\nPublished: 2013-10-19\n\nComments: 22 pages; Accepted October 2013 for publication in J. Combin. Math. Combin. Comput\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258082408_Complete_Mixed_Doubles_Round_Robin_Tournaments)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments ... Existence of self-orthogonal Latin squares ISOLS(6m+2,2m)\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\n  * October 2013\n\n  * Source\n  * arXiv\n\nAuthors:\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (15)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nIncomplete self-orthogonal latin squares\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Apr 2009\n\n  * Katherine Heinrich\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nWe show that for all n ≥ 3k + 1, n ≠ 6, there exists an incomplete self-orthogonal latin square of order n with an empty order k subarray, called an ISOLS(n;k), except perhaps when (n;k) ∈ {(6m + i;2m):i = 2, 6}.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nSelf-orthogonal latin squares (SOLS)\n\nChapter\n\nEarly examples of spouse avoidance\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2008\n\n  * Ian Anderson\n\nSpouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments were defined in 1973 by Brayton, Coppersmith and Hoffman. We discuss some earlier appearances of these designs in the late nineteenth century.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nBalanced equitable mixed doubles round-robin tournaments\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2013\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Douglas D. Smith\n\nView\n\nSelf-orthogonal Latin squares\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1976\n\n  * cite13†R. K. Brayton * Don Coppersmith\n  * A.J. Hoffman\n\nView\n\nA few more incomplete self-orthogonal Latin squares and related designs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2000\n\n  * R. Julian R. Abel\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * H. Zhang\n  * Ledoux Zhu\n\n\nExistence of self-orthogonal Latin squares ISOLS(6m+2,2m)\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1995\n\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nView\n\nSelf-orthogonal latin squares of all orders 𝑛≠2,3,6\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1974\n  * B AM MATH SOC\n\n  * R. K. Brayton\n  * Donald Coppersmith\n\nHoley Steiner pentagon systems\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1999\n  * J COMB DES\n\n  * R. Julian R. Abel\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * H. Zhang\n\n\nConstructing cyclic PBIBD(2)s through an optimization approach: Thirty‐two new cyclic designs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 2004\n  * J COMB DES\n\n  * Luis B. Morales\n\nWe formulate the construction of cyclic partially balanced incomplete block designs with two associate classes (PBIBD(2)s) as a combinatorial optimization problem. We propose an algorithm based on tabu search to tackle the problem. Our algorithm constructed 32 new cyclic PBIBD(2)s. © 2004 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.\n\n\nThe spectrum of HSOLSSOM(hn) where h is even\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 1996\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nIn this paper, we describe a generalized product construction and a construction using Steiner pentagon systems to obtain holey self-orthogonal Latin squares with symmetric orthogonal mates (HSOLSSOM). We investigate the existence of HSOLSSOM(hn) for even h. We first improve the known result for h = 2 and show that a HSOLSSOM(2n) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for n ∈ E = {8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 28, 32}. As a consequence, we then establish that for h  2 (mod 4) and h ⩾ 10 a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for nϵE. More conclusively, we show that for h  0 (mod 4), a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists if and only if n ⩾ 5. We are also able to apply our results to construct a unipotent SOLSSOM(62), the existence of which was previously unknown.\n\nExistence of frame SOLS of type\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2002\n\n  * Yunqing Xu\n  * Hantao Zhang\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nView\n\nORTHOGONAL LATIN SQUARES\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jul 1959\n  * P NATL ACAD SCI USA\n\n  * E. T. Parker\n\nView\n\n\n  * D R Berman\n  * D D Smith\n  * Mitchell Tournaments\n\nD.R. Berman and D.D. Smith, Mitchell tournaments, Bull. Inst. Combin. Appl. 65 (2012), 33-42.\n\nSome new HSOLSSOMs of type h n and 1 n u 1\n\n  * Jan 2001\n  * J COMB DES\n  * 435-444\n\n  * G Ge\n  * R J R Abel\n\nG. Ge and R.J.R. Abel, Some new HSOLSSOMs of type h n and 1 n u 1, J. Combin. Des. 9 (2001), 435-444.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1310.5240)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\nSubmitted 19 Oct 2013\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nUniversity of North Carolina Wilmington Qi Statistics Ltd.\n\nDR\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nIN\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$≥ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$≥ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{berman2013completemixeddoublesround, title={Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments}, author={David R. Berman and Ian N. Wakeling}, year={2013}, eprint={1310.5240}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5240}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDavid R. Berman - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/david-r-berman)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Starting from a nonorientable surface, the $G$-series is $012\\dot{4}60\\dot{3}$ according to increasing genus. ... Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\n## Papers by David R. Berman\n\n2 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2024-06-30 v2\n\nDavid R. Berman, Lee O. Leonard\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA 2-Person Game Decomposing 2-Manifolds\n\nTwo players play a game by alternately splitting a surface of a compact $2$-manifold along a simple closed curve that is not null-homotopic and attaching disks to the resulting boundary; the last player who can move wins. Starting from an orientable surface, the $G$-series is $01\\dot{2}\\dot{0}$ according to increasing genus. Starting from a nonorientable surface, the $G$-series is $012\\dot{4}60\\dot{3}$ according to increasing genus. Nim addition determines the $G$-values of the remaining compact $2$-manifolds.\n\n2013-10-19\n\nDavid R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\ntournament--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstructing Cyclic PBIBD(2)s through an Optimization Approach: Thirty Two New Cyclic Designs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/224941817_Constructing_Cyclic_PBIBD2s_through_an_Optimization_Approach_Thirty_Two_New_Cyclic_Designs)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments ... Our algorithm was able to construct 51 resolvable and 71 non-resolvable PBIBD(2)s.\nThe program fixes the partnerships and then exchanges them between games in a tabu search algorithm that seeks to optimize the opposition pairs incidence matrix (see [14]). The examples will be used in the next section as the basis of our recursive construction. ...\n\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 2013\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Ian N. Wakeling\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nConstruction of Super-Saturated Designs\n\nArticle\n\nConstructing some PBIBD(2)s by Tabu Search Algorithm\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Nov 2002\n\n  * Luis B. Morales\n\nSome papers have dealt with the construction of 2-concurrence designs as an optimization problem. In this paper we generalize these results to formulate also the construction of partially balanced incomplete block designs with 2 associate classes as a combinatorial optimization problem. We will propose an algorithm based on tabu search to construct many of these designs. Our algorithm was able to construct 51 resolvable and 71 non-resolvable PBIBD(2)s.\n\nView\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVolume 95, (2015) (https://combinatorialpress.com/jcmcc/vol95/)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; #### Complete mixed doubles round robin tournaments\n\nResearch Article\n\n#### Pre-hull number of Cartesian and strong graph products\n\nPeterin, Iztok\n\nJ. Comb. Math. Comb. Comput. 95, 55-64 (2015).\n\nResearch Article\n\n#### Complete mixed doubles round robin tournaments\n\nBerman, David R.; Wakeling, Ian N.\n\nJ. Comb. Math. Comb. Comput. 95, 65-86 (2015).\n\nResearch Article\n\n#### Distributions for transformations of derangements\n\nMihnea, Amy; Hoffman, Frederick\n\nJ. Comb. Math. Comb. Comput. 95, 87-98 (2015).\n\nResearch Article\n\n#### Edge domination in grids\n\nKlostermeyer, William F.; Yeo, Anders\n\nJ. Comb. Math. Comb. Comput. 95, 99-117 (2015).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy your round robin keeps giving you the same opponents | Ryan McDonald (https://ryanm.com/2026/09/27/why-your-round-robin-repeats-opponents/)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The event was an 18-player mixed doubles round robin with 8 rounds on 4 courts, so 16 players on court each round and 2 sitting out. ... The scheduler already generated 100 complete candidate schedules and kept the best one. ... The count is the double factorial (2C-1)!!, and it grows the way double factorials grow.\n\n# Why your round robin keeps giving you the same opponents\n\nEngineering  · 27 Sep 2026\n\nIf you run round robins, you have probably heard someone say “didn’t we just play these guys?” For a long time I assumed that was just what happens in a small event. Then I measured it, and it turned out the app was causing it, for a reason I should have caught earlier.\n\n## What I measured\n\nThe event was an 18-player mixed doubles round robin with 8 rounds on 4 courts, so 16 players on court each round and 2 sitting out.\n\nTeammate uniqueness was perfect. Nobody partnered with the same person twice, which is the hard constraint in the algorithm, and it held every time.\n\nOpponents were a different story. 16 pairs of players faced each other twice in a single night. For that configuration, a good schedule can put almost everyone against everyone else exactly once, so this was a real problem and not a rounding error. Players notice it, and it makes an 8-round night feel like a 4-round night.\n\n## What the algorithm was doing\nScoring is now lexicographic, so it minimizes repeat teammates first and then, among schedules tied on that, minimizes repeat opponents.\n\n## The part organizers can actually use\n\nDigging through this turned up something more useful than the fix itself. A lot of repeat opponents aren’t bugs at all. They’re arithmetic. Past a certain point, no algorithm can help, because the schedule you asked for doesn’t exist.\n\nFor mixed doubles, the rules of thumb are roughly:\n\n  * If rounds are less than or equal to players per gender, everyone can meet at most once.\n  * If rounds are less than or equal to players per gender times two, everyone meets at most twice, which is fine and nobody complains.\n  * If rounds exceed players per gender times two, some pairs will meet three or more times no matter what.\n\nThe sweet spot is 16 players and 8 rounds, where every pair meets exactly once.\n\nSo if you’re running 8 rounds with 12 people, the repeats you’re seeing were guaranteed before anyone opened the app. You don’t need better software. You need one fewer round or four more players. Dink+ now warns you when your configuration is in that corner, which turned out to be a more useful feature than making the solver a few percent smarter.\n\n## What I took from it\n\nThe matchmaking logic itself was never wrong. Both bugs were about sequencing. One was committing to court 1 before knowing what it would cost courts 2 through 4, and the other was letting an evaluation write to the state it was evaluating against. The domain logic was fine, and the order of operations was doing the damage.\n\nThe fix that mattered most wasn’t the exhaustive search or the multi-start. It was three lines in the selection step, using a number the code had been carefully computing and then discarding for months. It’s worth asking of your own code what you’re already measuring and not looking at.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCMDRR: a step to change the community | PreventionWeb (https://www.preventionweb.net/publication/documents-and-publications/cmdrr-step-change-community)\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.2 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; It documents the copying strategies of the communities, their disaster risk reduction knowledge, as well as the good practices and process followed during the implementation of community-managed disaster risk reduction (CMDRR) project.\n\n# CMDRR: a step to change the community\n\nSource\n\nCaritas Internationalis\n\nThis book presents the disaster risk reduction efforts and coping mechanisms of the vulnerable communities which are part of the Strengthening the Community for Disaster Risk Management (SCDRM) project area in Bangladesh. Rather than a collection of stories, it focuses on facts, highlighting the disaster situations too many people are in, and the measures they take to save their lives, livelihoods and belongings. It documents the copying strategies of the communities, their disaster risk reduction knowledge, as well as the good practices and process followed during the implementation of community-managed disaster risk reduction (CMDRR) project.\n\n## Download\n\nAccess View full document English\n\nLast checked: 18 December 2019\n\n## Explore further\n\nThemes Capacity development Community-based DRR\n\nCountry and region Bangladesh\n\nNumber of pages\n\n105 p.\n\nPublication year\n\n2012\n\nPlease note: Content is displayed as last posted by a PreventionWeb community member or editor. The views expressed therein are not necessarily those of UNDRR, PreventionWeb, or its sponsors. See our terms of use--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJournal articles: 'Round-robin tournaments' – Grafiati (https://www.grafiati.com/en/literature-selections/round-robin-tournaments/journal/)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; The problem of scheduling a spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round-robin tennis tournament (SMDRTT) of order involves finding a playing schedule for married couples in such a way that no player teams up with his/her spouse, no player opposes his/her spouse, each player opposes every other player of the same sex exactly once, each player teams up with every player of the opposite sex (except his/her spouse) exactly once, and each player opposes every player of the opposite sex (except his/her spouse) exactly once. ... If the season cannot resume due to an external shock such as the coronavirus pandemic in 2020, the ranking of the teams becomes non-trivial: it is necessary to account for schedule imbalances and possibly for the different number of matches played. ... Algorithms A is that perfect matching is determined by using edge coloring of edge-matrix ; Algorithm B is that perfect matching is determined by partitioning edge-matrix into sub matrix and also by solving perfect matching of a complete graph .\n15\n\nEggar, Michael Hugh. \"On the organisation of round robin tournaments with constraints.\" ANZIAM Journal 55 (October 3, 2014): 357. http://dx.doi.org/10.21914/anziamj.v55i0.7244.\n\nFull text\n\nAdd to bibliography\n\nAPA, Harvard, Vancouver, ISO, and other styles\n\n16\n\nBurger, A. P., and J. H. Van Vuuren. \"Scheduling spouse - avoiding mixed doubles round - robin tennis tournaments.\" Suid-Afrikaanse Tydskrif vir Natuurwetenskap en Tegnologie 28, no. 1 (2009): 35–63. http://dx.doi.org/10.4102/satnt.v28i1.47.\n\nFull text\n\nAbstract:\n\nThe problem of scheduling a spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round-robin tennis tournament (SMDRTT) of order involves finding a playing schedule for married couples in such a way that no player teams up with his/her spouse, no player opposes his/her spouse, each player opposes every other player of the same sex exactly once, each player teams up with every player of the opposite sex (except his/her spouse) exactly once, and each player opposes every player of the opposite sex (except his/her spouse) exactly once.\n\n17\n\nBriskorn, Dirk. \"Combinatorial properties of strength groups in round robin tournaments.\" European Journal of Operational Research 192, no. 3 (2009): 744–54. http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ejor.2007.10.007.\n\nFull text\n\nAdd to bibliography\n\nAPA, Harvard, Vancouver, ISO, and other styles\n\n18\n\nEGGAR, M. H. \"ON THE ORGANIZATION OF ROUND ROBIN TOURNAMENTS WITH CONSTRAINTS.\" ANZIAM Journal 55, no. 4 (2014): 357–61. http://dx.doi.org/10.1017/s1446181114000108.\n\nFull text\n\nAbstract:\n\nAbstractA sporting league places every team into one of several divisions of equal size, and runs a round robin tournament for each division. Some teams are paired with another team, not necessarily in the same division, to share facilities. It is shown that however many teams are paired and whatever the pairings, it is always possible to schedule the fixtures in the minimum time, so that no two paired teams have home matches simultaneously.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(CMDR